<Base lang="en" title="eSathi Status Check: How to Check eDistrict UP Application Status Online" description="Check your eSathi status online — track an eDistrict UP application with your SRN without login, what every status means and what to do next, certificate verification, safe download, pending payments and complaint escalation." path="/en/status-verification/" altPath="/status-verification/" faqs={faqs} updated="14 November 2026" datePublished="2026-10-23" dateModified="2026-11-14" howto={howto}>
<div class="content-col"><h1 class="page-title">Application Status & Certificate Verification</h1><p class="note">Note: This is an independent information guide — it is not the official portal (edistrict.up.gov.in).</p><p class="updated-line">The correct route for tracking, verification and download</p>
<div class="action-grid"><a class="action-card" href="https://edistrict.up.gov.in/" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><strong>Official eDistrict portal ↗</strong><span>Open status or verification</span></a><a class="action-card" href="https://esathi.up.gov.in/citizenservices/login/login.aspx" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><strong>Official citizen login ↗</strong><span>Download an issued document</span></a></div>
<section class="box"><h2>Choose the right task</h2><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Keep ready</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Track progress</td><td>Your SRN (Service Request Number)</td><td>Application Status</td></tr><tr><td>Authenticate a certificate</td><td>The Certificate Number printed on the certificate</td><td>Certificate Verification</td></tr><tr><td>Download final PDF</td><td>Citizen credentials</td><td>Issued/Completed Applications</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="note">The SRN and the Certificate Number are different numbers and are not interchangeable: the SRN is the tracking number you receive when you submit an application and is used only for checking status — it cannot download or verify anything. The Certificate Number is printed on the final certificate and is what verification uses; entering the SRN on the verification page returns no result. Follow the official field label on the portal.</div></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Check status without login: step by step</h2><p>After submitting an application, the most common question is "how far has my certificate reached?" The good news: checking the status does not require a login — the SRN number you received at application time is enough.</p><ol><li><strong>Open the official portal</strong> — go to the homepage of edistrict.up.gov.in. The status facility is given on the homepage itself; you do not need to enter an account.</li><li><strong>Choose "Application Status"</strong> — this option on the homepage opens the form where the SRN is entered.</li><li><strong>Enter the SRN exactly</strong> — this is the number that arrived by SMS as soon as the application was submitted, and it is also printed on the receipt. Check it digit by digit — even one wrong digit returns no result.</li><li><strong>Fill the captcha and search</strong> — enter the captcha code and press search. Watch the letter case (upper/lower) in the captcha and keep the browser zoom normal.</li><li><strong>Read the status and the remark</strong> — the screen shows the current status together with the date, department, pending officer and remark. Do not read the status word alone — the remark tells you where the file currently is and whether you need to do anything.</li><li><strong>Save the result privately</strong> — keep a screenshot or note with yourself; do not post your application number or personal data in a public group.</li></ol><h3>Checking status after login</h3><p>If you want to watch several applications at once, logging in to eSathi is more convenient. The dashboard lists all your applications with their SRN, certificate type, application date and current status, and any application can be opened to see its details. If you cannot log in — forgotten password or OTP not arriving — see the <a href="/en/blog/esathi-login-forgot-password-otp/">login troubleshooting guide</a>.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>What every status means — and what to do next</h2><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th><th>What you should do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Submitted</th><td>The application has reached the system successfully.</td><td>Keep the receipt and SRN safe; the application is then assigned to the concerned officer.</td></tr><tr><th>Under Verification</th><td>Departmental or field checks are underway — usually the longest stage.</td><td>Read the remark shown with it. Keep someone present at the address so the field check can be completed.</td></tr><tr><th>Query/Correction</th><td>A small defect or mistake was found in the application, which must be fixed and sent again.</td><td>Log in, read the correction note, fix the exact defect and resubmit where the option is offered — the SRN stays the same.</td></tr><tr><th>Approved</th><td>The application has been approved; the final document may still be getting generated.</td><td>Wait a while and check the status again — download works only once it shows "Certificate Generated".</td></tr><tr><th>Certificate Generated</th><td>The digitally signed certificate is ready and available for download.</td><td>Download the signed document from Completed Applications — see the download section below.</td></tr><tr><th>Rejected</th><td>The application was refused because of some defect.</td><td>Log in and read the rejection reason, then correct the defect. File a fresh application only when the portal or issuing authority says so — do not file a duplicate without reading the remark.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="note">No universal timeline applies to every service; the statutory time limit differs by service. That is why this page makes no fixed-day promise for any status — the remark and the pending officer are the most reliable information you have.</div></section>
<section class="box"><h2>The journey your application takes inside</h2><p>A status is easier to understand when you know where the file travels. You submit the application → the system assigns it to the Lekhpal of your area (in rural areas) or the Revenue Inspector (in urban areas) → the Lekhpal/Inspector carries out the field check of your address and documents → the check report moves forward (a stage such as "Lekhpal report awaited" belongs here) → the file reaches the Tehsildar ("forwarded to Tehsildar") → the Tehsildar signs the certificate with their Digital Signature (DSC) → the certificate is issued. So if "Under Verification" stays for a long time, the file is usually at the field-check stage — read the remark before worrying.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Common problems and fixes</h2><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SRN/application number lost</td><td>Look for the SMS received at application time — the SRN is in it. Log in to eSathi; the dashboard lists all applications together with their SRN. If you applied through a CSC, the SRN is printed on the centre receipt — if you do not have it, or it is not readable, ask the centre for a readable receipt. Never leave a centre without the receipt.</td></tr><tr><td>No result after entering the number</td><td>Recheck the number digit by digit — one wrong digit returns no result. Also confirm you are entering the SRN on the status page; the SRN does not work on the verification page, which needs the Certificate Number.</td></tr><tr><td>The same status for a long time</td><td>First read the remark and the pending officer — that shows where the file is held up. Field verification is usually the longest stage. If the delay is still unreasonable, complain through the escalation route below; if a deadline is close, also see the <a href="/en/blog/janhit-guarantee-appeal-delayed-certificate/">Janhit Guarantee Act appeal process</a>.</td></tr><tr><td>Portal does not open, or a server error appears</td><td>Use the Chrome browser, clear the cache, turn off the ad-blocker and try an incognito window. On mobile, switch on "Desktop site" mode. If the session keeps expiring, remember the portal ends the session after a period of inactivity — keep all details ready beforehand so the work finishes quickly.</td></tr><tr><td>Cannot log in, so status/download is stuck</td><td>The no-login status check above still works. Download does need a login — password reset, forgotten login ID and OTP problems are solved step by step in the <a href="/en/blog/esathi-login-forgot-password-otp/">login troubleshooting post</a>.</td></tr></tbody></table></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Verify an issued certificate</h2><ol><li>Open Certificate Verification from the official homepage.</li><li>Enter the Certificate Number printed on the certificate, and any other identifiers the form asks for, exactly as labelled.</li><li>Compare name, issuing authority, date and details.</li><li>Confirm that any QR code opens a government domain.</li><li>Take mismatches to the issuing tehsil/registrar.</li></ol><div class="warn">No record alone does not prove fraud; a wrong field, migrated record or outage can cause it.</div><p>Verification never needs a login — an institution checks your certificate with the Certificate Number or QR code alone, so never share your login ID, password or OTP with anyone in the name of verification. The complete print, download and verification process is covered in the <a href="/en/blog/certificate-print-download-verification/">certificate print, download and verification post</a>.</p><p>The national <a href="https://dc.crsorgi.gov.in/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Civil Registration System</a> lists Uttar Pradesh among supported states for birth/death records.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Download and store safely</h2><p>Download has one precondition: the status must show <strong>Certificate Generated</strong>. If it still shows Approved, wait for the signed PDF to be generated and check the status again. Download happens only after login — the full route is given in the <a href="/en/blog/esathi-download/">eSathi download post</a>.</p><ul class="checklist"><li>Use the final signed document from Issued/Completed Applications.</li><li>Prefer the portal download control to browser print.</li><li>Do not edit the original PDF; keep protected cloud and offline backups.</li><li>Check the receiving institution's issue-date and format rules.</li><li>Rely on DigiLocker only when the record appears from the official issuer.</li></ul></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Payment pending or duplicate debit</h2><ol><li>Save the bank reference, portal receipt, date, amount and a screenshot.</li><li>Do not pay again immediately; watch the reconciliation status.</li><li>Follow the portal's retry or refund instruction.</li><li>If it stays unresolved, give the transaction reference to the helpdesk and the bank.</li></ol></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Delay and complaint escalation</h2><p>In a complaint, write the applicant, service, application number, submission date, current status and a concise description of the problem. First take a grievance reference; then give the same to the helpdesk or issuing office. If there is a deadline, attach the notice. Verify the statutory time limit and appellate authority from the service-specific notification — assuming a universal 7–15 days for every service is not correct.</p><p>Official district pages publish the helpline <strong>0522-2304706</strong> and <strong>ceghelpdesk@gmail.com</strong> as the helpdesk. Never share your OTP or password.</p></section>
